-- Passage rg9-theme-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c542e562-63e4-56f5-9fec-55eb5210faad', 'rg9-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Green Notebook$q$, $q$Tomas Arriaga had won eleven games in a row at the Thursday chess club, and he had started to walk in like someone arriving at his own party. He set up his pieces quickly, played quickly, and left before the club's sponsor, Mrs. Adeyemi, could ask him to help put the boards away.

Then a sixth grader named Lin Chao sat down across from him. She was small, quiet, and slow. She spent four minutes on her first move while Tomas tapped his fingers on the table. Forty minutes later, he was staring at a position where every one of his moves lost something.

He resigned and pushed back his chair. "Lucky," he said, though he knew it wasn't.

Mrs. Adeyemi caught him at the door and handed him a thin green notebook. "Write down that game," she said. "Every move. Then write what you were thinking."

"I wasn't thinking anything. I was just playing."

"That," she said, "is the part worth writing down."

At home he tried to rebuild the game from memory. He got stuck around move fourteen, where Lin had moved a knight to the edge of the board, a move he had laughed at. Replaying it slowly, he saw that the knight had been aimed at a square three moves ahead. He had not lost because Lin was lucky. He had lost because he had stopped looking.

He filled six pages. In his eleven wins, he realized, he had never once written anything down, because there had seemed to be nothing to learn.

The next Thursday he arrived early and asked Lin for a rematch. He lost again, more slowly this time. Afterward, the two of them stayed behind with the green notebook open between them, arguing happily about move fourteen while Mrs. Adeyemi stacked the boards around them.$q$, 'draft')
