-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('71faa34b-bb27-563d-a6f9-253341a2d3a5', 'rg9-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Skin That Changes Color$q$, $q$Few animals can disappear as quickly as a cuttlefish. One moment it hovers over a sandy patch of sea floor, pale and speckled. A second later, it has shifted to the dark, blotchy pattern of the nearby rocks and become nearly invisible to a passing fish.

The secret lies in the animal's skin. Like human skin, it has more than one layer. The outer layer, called the epidermis, is thin and clear. Beneath it lies the dermis, a thicker layer that holds nerves, muscles and thousands of tiny color cells.

These cells, called chromatophores, are small elastic sacs filled with red, yellow, brown or black pigment. Each sac is ringed by muscles connected to the animal's nervous system. When the muscles pull outward, the sac stretches wide and its color spreads across a larger patch of skin. When the muscles relax, the sac shrinks to a dot that is hard to see. Because the brain controls the muscles directly, a cuttlefish can change its whole body in less than a second.

Other cells beneath the chromatophores reflect light, adding shimmering blues and greens that the pigments alone cannot make. Together, these layers let the animal produce stripes, spots and moving waves of color.

What puzzles researchers is that most cuttlefish appear to be color-blind. Their eyes seem to have only one type of light-sensitive cell, which should make it hard for them to tell colors apart. Some scientists suspect that the skin itself may be sensitive to light, helping the animal match its surroundings. The question is still open, and laboratories around the world are testing different explanations.$q$, 'draft')
