-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0b0f8aee-e3a6-5cc5-afe9-bac2868278d6', 'rg9-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Century Ride$q$, $q$Kwame had trained all summer for the century, a one-hundred-mile ride that started at dawn from the county fairgrounds. His aunt Efua had ridden it six times. This year, for the first time, she had agreed to let him ride beside her.

The start was crowded. Hundreds of cyclists rolled out in a slow, bright river of helmets and jerseys, their wheels ticking softly as they waited for the road to open up. Kwame's legs felt fresh, and for the first thirty miles he kept drifting ahead of his aunt, then circling back.

"Save it," Efua told him. "The hills come after lunch."

She was right. By mile sixty, the road tilted upward and stayed that way. Kwame shifted into his easiest gear and pushed down on the pedals in a steady rhythm, counting strokes to keep his mind off the burning in his thighs. Efua stayed just behind him, close enough that he could hear her breathing.

The afternoon faded faster than he expected. By the time they reached the last rest stop, the sun was low, and Efua reached down to flip a switch on her front wheel. A small lamp glowed. "Dynamo," she said, seeing his face. "The wheel makes the power. As long as you keep turning, you have light."

The final miles were flat. A tailwind came up from the south, and the two riders felt themselves propelled along the empty road as if someone were pushing them. When the fairground lights appeared, Kwame realized he was grinning. He had not looked at his bike computer in an hour, and he did not need to. He knew they had made it.$q$, 'draft')
