-- Passage rg9-word-parts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('70e536f5-ef5f-5631-8450-c7ac7df9f16a', 'rg9-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why the Straw Looks Broken$q$, $q$Place a straw in a clear glass of water and look at it from the side. The straw seems to snap at the surface, as if the part under the water had shifted sideways. Lift it out, and it is perfectly straight. Nothing happened to the straw. Something happened to the light.

Light travels at different speeds through different materials. It moves fastest through empty space, a little slower through air and noticeably slower through water or glass. When a beam crosses from air into water at an angle, one side of the beam slows down before the other, and the whole beam bends. Scientists say the light has been refracted, and that bending is what makes the straw appear broken.

You can see the same effect with a rectangular glass block, the kind used in many science classrooms. Shine a narrow beam into one flat side, and inspect the beam carefully as it passes through. It bends once on the way in and again on the way out.

Refraction is not the same as reflection. When light strikes a mirror, it is not slowed down and passed through; it is deflected back toward the side it came from, like a ball bouncing off a wall.

A glass prism shows refraction at its most colorful. Each color of light bends by a slightly different amount, so white light spreads into a band of red, orange, yellow, green, blue and violet. Some light lies beyond what the eye can detect. Just past the red end sits infrared light, which we cannot see but can feel as warmth on our skin.$q$, 'draft')
