-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ebc360fc-3e45-5a02-8f90-48b2cd3cddcd', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let the Stars Back In$q$, $q$From an opinion column by Teodora Valdes, a retired pharmacist and amateur astronomer, published in the Pine Hollow community newsletter.

When was the last time you saw the Milky Way? If you grew up in Pine Hollow forty years ago, you probably saw it every clear summer night, a pale river of light stretching from the water tower to the ridge. If you are under twenty, you may never have seen it here at all.

The stars have not gone anywhere. We have simply drowned them out. Our streetlights, parking lots, and porch lamps throw much of their light sideways and upward, where it lights nothing but the sky. Astronomers call this skyglow, and in our town it has grown worse every decade.

I understand why people like bright lights. They feel safe, and no one wants to stumble on a dark sidewalk. I am not asking anyone to turn off a single light. I am asking that we point our lights down. Shielded fixtures put the light on the ground, where people actually walk, and they often use less electricity to do it. Several towns in our region have already made the switch and report lower energy bills.

There is more at stake than a pretty view. Migrating birds use the stars to navigate, and bright skies can pull them off course. Moths, bats, and the animals that feed on them all depend on darkness. Our own bodies, too, are built for a rhythm of day and night.

Next month, the council will consider replacing the old streetlights on Main Street with shielded ones. I hope you will come and speak in favor. Bring your children. Tell them that the sky above their own town once held thousands of stars, and that it can again.

Imagine standing on the ball field on an August night and seeing what your grandparents saw. That is not a luxury. It is part of our town's inheritance, and we can reclaim it simply by aiming our lights where they belong.$q$, 'draft')
