   $q$Which statement best describes how the author's appeals change across the last three paragraphs?$q$, $q$[{"text":"She moves from an emotional appeal about children to a purely technical discussion of electricity costs.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"She moves from facts about birds, wildlife, and health to an emotional appeal involving children and grandparents.","is_correct":true},{"text":"She uses the wildlife facts to argue that animals matter more than the safety of town residents.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"She relies only on her authority as a former pharmacist and offers no reasons beyond her opinion.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-evidence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$She moves from facts about birds, wildlife, and health to an emotional appeal involving children and grandparents.$q$, $q$The paragraph beginning "There is more at stake" gives factual reasons about birds, moths, and body rhythms, and the closing paragraphs appeal to feeling with children, grandparents, and "our town's inheritance." Electricity costs appear earlier, in the fourth paragraph, so the reversed order is wrong.$q$, 'draft', 'ebc360fc-3e45-5a02-8f90-48b2cd3cddcd')
