  ('e142a2c6-0699-5a00-82da-679e9f3ba6f2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null, 'hard',
   $q$How does the final paragraph use language to reinforce the author's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It calls a restored quilt \"a kind of costume\" and ends with paired short sentences that rename the flaws as history rather than damage.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It uses technical terms from conservation science to prove that faded dyes cannot be safely brightened by any known method.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-language"},{"text":"It admits a restored quilt \"would photograph beautifully\" and then concludes that the museum should create a restored copy for display.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It ends with paired short sentences that warn visitors not to touch the quilt, since human hands are what damaged it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It calls a restored quilt "a kind of costume" and ends with paired short sentences that rename the flaws as history rather than damage.$q$, $q$The costume metaphor suggests restoration would be a disguise, and "That is not damage. That is history." redefines what visitors see as flaws. The concession about photographs leads to the costume point, not to a plan for a copy.$q$, 'draft', '6c81c09c-9abe-5798-b04e-1be1002118ed')
