-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4ddd23c7-aaa2-5e4e-917c-d7bc7d910d16', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Runners at the Back$q$, $q$From remarks by Coach Adaeze Okafor at the end-of-season banquet for the Ridgemont High School cross-country team.

Tonight we will hand out medals to our fastest runners, and they deserve every one. Priya broke the course record at Elm Lake. Mateo finished fourth at the regional meet. I am proud of them, and I hope you will cheer loudly when they come up.

But I want to spend a few minutes talking about the runners you did not see on the results board.

Do you know who was at practice at six in the morning in late August, when the fog was so thick we could barely see the first mile marker? Do you know who ran the hill repeats on Carver Road after everyone else had finished, because she promised herself she would not walk this time? Do you know who stayed after every home meet to collect the course flags? They were not our fastest runners. Most of them finished in the back half of every race.

Some people think a team is only as good as its top five. On the scoring sheet, that is true. In every other way, it is wrong. A team is the people who show up, and our slowest runners showed up more than anyone. They cheered at the finish line long after their own races were over. They learned the names of runners from other schools. They made this the kind of team new students want to join.

Here is what I have learned in eighteen years of coaching. Speed comes and goes. A pulled muscle can take it away in an afternoon. Character stays. The habit of finishing what you start, of running the last mile as hard as the first, of caring about the person behind you, will last long after your best time has been forgotten.

So when we read the names of the runners who earned our Spirit Award, I ask you to stand. They will not be the fastest people in this room. They will be the reason this room is full.$q$, 'draft')
