-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ee5f3edf-399d-5092-a219-43ac8edf1a97', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Does This App Deserve to Interrupt You?$q$, $q$From an essay by Farah Siddiqui, a software designer, written for a magazine read by high school computer science students.

I design apps for a living, so let me tell you a secret about my profession. Many of the apps on your phone are built to interrupt you. Each buzz, badge, and banner is designed to pull your attention back to the screen, because an app that is opened more often looks more successful to the company that made it.

I am not saying notifications are evil. A message telling you that your bus is running late is genuinely useful. An alert that your friend needs a ride is worth an interruption. The problem is that these useful alerts arrive mixed in with dozens of others: a game reminding you to collect a daily reward, a shopping app announcing a sale, a video app suggesting something you did not ask to watch.

Researchers who study attention have found that even brief interruptions make it harder to return to a demanding task. If you are halfway through a chemistry problem and your phone lights up, the cost is not just the five seconds you spend looking. It is the minutes it takes to remember where you were.

So here is my challenge. Open your settings tonight and look at every app that is allowed to notify you. For each one, ask a simple question: does this app deserve to interrupt me? If the answer is no, switch it off. Most people who try this keep fewer than a third of their notifications.

Many of you will become the people who design the next generation of software. When you do, I hope you will remember what it felt like to take your attention back. Build apps that respect the people who use them. Build apps that earn attention instead of stealing it. The best technology does not demand to be noticed; it waits until it is needed.$q$, 'draft')
