-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2fc06731-7ede-5214-b1b1-e08f9aa49554', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Place to Sit$q$, $q$From a column by Nomvula Dlamini, a retired city planner, in a neighborhood newspaper.

There is a simple test for whether a street was designed for people: try to find a place to sit. On too many of our streets, you will walk for blocks without finding a single bench. You will find parking meters, trash cans, and signs telling you where not to stand, but nowhere to rest.

This might sound like a small complaint. It is not. For a teenager waiting for a bus, a missing bench is an inconvenience. For an eighty-year-old woman carrying groceries home, it can decide whether she walks to the store at all. For a parent with a tired toddler, it can decide whether the family visits the park or stays home. A bench is not decoration. It is the difference between a street people can use and a street they avoid.

Some business owners worry that benches attract loitering. I understand the concern, and I spent thirty years listening to it at public meetings. But in the neighborhoods where we added seating, the opposite happened. More people stopped. More people lingered near shop windows. Several store owners who had opposed the benches later asked us for more.

Benches are also among the cheapest improvements a city can make. One costs less than repaving a few feet of road, and a well-built bench lasts twenty years or more.

I am asking the city to set a modest goal: a place to sit on every block of our main shopping streets within five years. That is not a grand plan. It will not win design awards. But it will tell every resident, young and old, that this city expects them to stay a while.$q$, 'draft')
