   $q$How does the comparison of quitting to "training for a race and stopping just before the starting line" advance the author's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It suggests that learning a language is a competition, so students should stay only if they can beat their classmates.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It suggests that the third year is the hardest part of the program, so only the most athletic students should continue.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-analogy"},{"text":"It suggests that students should join a sports team instead of a language class, since both require steady training.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"off-topic"},{"text":"It suggests that leaving after two years wastes the effort already spent just as the reward arrives, supporting his closing appeal.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It suggests that leaving after two years wastes the effort already spent just as the reward arrives, supporting his closing appeal.$q$, $q$In the analogy, training is the "hard, repetitive" first two years and the race is the third year, where the payoff comes; this sets up his final request to think about "what you have already invested." Nothing in the text says language learning is a contest.$q$, 'draft', 'f863236c-afb5-59f4-b253-59d685a12316')
