-- Passage rg10-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('99e804a9-6100-51fa-b6d6-fb4fef3eb4f3', 'rg10-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Give the Vulture Its Due$q$, $q$From an essay by wildlife biologist Ines Carvalho for the newsletter of the Saddleback Ridge Nature Center.

Few animals have a worse reputation than the vulture. In cartoons, vultures circle over lost travelers in the desert. In everyday speech, calling someone a vulture is an insult. Even many people who love birds admit they find vultures ugly, with their bare heads and hunched shoulders.

I would like to make the case that the vulture is one of the most useful animals in our region, and one of the least appreciated.

Vultures eat animals that have already died. That habit is exactly why people find them unpleasant, and it is exactly why we need them. A carcass left on the ground can spread bacteria that sicken other animals, including livestock and pets. Vultures clean it up quickly, often within hours. Their stomachs contain acids so strong that they destroy most of the germs they swallow. In effect, they are a free sanitation crew that works every day of the year.

Even the features we find ugly have a purpose. A bare head stays cleaner than a feathered one when a bird feeds on a carcass. The long, slow circling that looks so gloomy in movies is actually an efficient way to ride rising columns of warm air while searching for food with very little effort.

In parts of the world where vulture populations have crashed, the results have been costly. Carcasses lie uneaten for days, wild dog populations grow, and diseases spread more easily. We should not wait to learn that lesson ourselves.

So the next time you see a dark shape circling high above the ridge, I hope you will look again. You are not watching a symbol of bad luck. You are watching one of nature's most dedicated workers, doing a job that no one else wants.$q$, 'draft')
