-- Passage rg10-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5181c286-dfeb-56ae-b916-e39709050393', 'rg10-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Counting Fire$q$, $q$The meteor shower was supposed to peak at two in the morning, and by midnight eleven members of the Westbrook High science club were lying on blankets in the soccer field with their faces turned to the sky. Tomas Ferreira held the clipboard. As data recorder, his job was simple: every time someone called out a meteor, he made a tally mark in the box for that ten-minute window, and at dawn he would enter the totals into a university's public database.

Tomas wanted the totals to be big. The school that logged the most complete observations this season would win a visit from a planetary scientist, and he had already pictured himself showing her around the lab. He also wanted, more quietly, to be the kind of person a scientist would trust.

At ten past one, a bank of clouds slid over the field from the west. The calls stopped. Ten minutes passed, then twenty, then forty, with nothing but the hum of the stadium lights in the distance.

Adaeze Nwosu, the club president, rolled over on her blanket. "Just estimate it," she said. "We were getting about nine every ten minutes before the clouds. Write nine in each box. Everyone does it, and the meteors were still up there."

Tomas looked at the empty boxes. He penciled a small 9 into the first one, then a second, then stopped with the pencil resting on the third. Adaeze was not wrong that the meteors had kept falling. But the form asked what they had seen, not what had probably happened.

When the clouds cleared at two, the calls began again, and Tomas filled the boxes faster than he could count aloud. At dawn, while the others folded blankets, he erased the two nines and wrote across the gap in careful letters: Clouded over, no observations.

Adaeze read it over his shoulder. For a moment she said nothing, and he braced for an argument. Then she took the pencil from his hand and signed her initials beside his note.$q$, 'draft')
