-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('797ac378-440f-52e7-b9a8-fb1b3cd32b1e', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Rivals and a Shared Problem$q$, $q$Text 1: The Two Millers of Varn (an invented legend)

In the valley of Varn, the legend says, two millers named Arvo and Benedek built their mills on opposite banks of the same stream. For years they competed for every farmer's grain, lowering prices and spreading rumors about each other's flour. Then came a summer so dry that the stream shrank to a trickle, too weak to turn either wheel.

Arvo dug a channel to pull the water toward his own mill, and Benedek did the same, and soon neither wheel had enough water to move. The farmers' grain sat in sacks while the harvest season passed. At last the two men met in the middle of the dry streambed. Together they built a single narrow flume that carried all the water to one wheel, and they agreed to grind on alternate days. Both mills survived the drought. Ever after, the people of Varn said that a shared wheel turns when two divided wheels stand still.

Text 2: Shared Trail (a story)

I joined the Pell Ridge cross-country team in September, and the first thing I learned was that we were supposed to dislike Eastbrook. Both schools trained on the Hollins Creek trail, and both teams acted as if the other was trespassing. Our captain timed their runners from behind the trees. Their coach once complained that we left the trail markers crooked.

Then October rain washed mud and fallen branches across the middle mile. Our team cleared the stretch nearest our school, and Eastbrook cleared the stretch nearest theirs. Neither of us touched the steep part in between, so neither team could run a full loop. For two weeks we jogged back and forth on half a trail, bored and slow.

Finally our captain, Priya Castellanos, walked to the washed-out section with a rake, and Eastbrook's captain, Femi Adeyemi, showed up with a wheelbarrow. I was there because Priya needed someone to carry the shovels. Watching the two of them agree on a plan felt like seeing two cats decide to share a windowsill. Both teams worked that Saturday, and afterward the coaches set up a schedule so the teams practiced on alternate afternoons and checked the trail together after storms.

We still race to beat Eastbrook, and we still cheer loudly when we do. But now, when I pass their runners at the trailhead, we nod. I think that counts as a kind of truce.$q$, 'draft')
