   $q$What does the author of Text 2 suggest by keeping the rivalry alive ("We still race to beat Eastbrook")?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Competition matters more than cooperation, so the trail repair was a minor event in the story.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misses-theme"},{"text":"The narrator regrets helping Eastbrook and wishes the trail had stayed blocked for their runners.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The cooperation in Text 2 failed, which shows the author believes the legend's lesson is mistaken.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Cooperating over a shared resource does not have to erase competition, a more nuanced version of the legend's lesson.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Cooperating over a shared resource does not have to erase competition, a more nuanced version of the legend's lesson.$q$, $q$The teams still race hard but now nod and check the trail together, so rivalry and cooperation exist side by side. The repair worked, so the choice saying the cooperation failed is not supported.$q$, 'draft', '797ac378-440f-52e7-b9a8-fb1b3cd32b1e')
