-- Passage rg10-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9fe5a644-2b65-5478-ade8-6a0ab6a9abf8', 'rg10-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Marsh That Came Back$q$, $q$In the 1890s, farmers along the fictional Fennick Bay built a long earthen wall, called a dike, to keep the tide off their land. Behind the dike, the salt marsh dried out, and for a few decades the farms grew good crops of oats and hay.

The success did not last. Marsh soil is mostly packed plant matter, and once it dried, it began to break down and shrink. Over sixty years the fields sank more than a meter, which left them lower than the sea outside the dike. Salt water seeped up from below, the crops failed, and by the 1950s most of the farms had been abandoned.

In 2009, an ecologist named Amara Okonjo proposed cutting three openings in the old dike and letting the tide back in. Her plan drew sharp criticism at first. For two years the flooded fields looked like bare gray mud, and several residents called the project a waste of public money.

Then the marsh began to rebuild itself. Each high tide carried a thin layer of silt onto the fields, and cordgrass took root in the new mud. The grass slowed the water, which made it drop even more silt, so the ground rose faster each year. Young fish and crabs, which shelter in marsh channels, returned in numbers the bay had not seen in a century.

The strongest test came in 2019, when a winter storm pushed an unusually high tide toward the town of Fennick. The restored marsh spread out and slowed the surge, and the water stopped short of the main street. Officials who had doubted Okonjo's plan now point out that the land the farmers once fought to drain has become the town's best protection from the sea.$q$, 'draft')
