-- Passage rg10-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bc864218-4c8c-52e6-bde0-588203f3f66e', 'rg10-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When the Clocks Agreed$q$, $q$Two centuries ago, every town in the fictional country of Varland kept its own time. Noon was the moment the sun stood highest over the town square, so a clock in the eastern port of Ostrel ran about twenty minutes ahead of a clock in the western hill town of Breck. Since people rarely traveled more than a day's walk, the difference mattered to almost no one.

The railway changed that. When the Ostrel and Breck line opened in 1851, its timetable had to list arrivals in the local time of each of its fourteen stations. Passengers missed connections, freight sat waiting on sidings, and station agents spent hours converting one town's time into another's.

A young timetable clerk named Teodor Abara proposed a simple fix: every station would follow the clock in Ostrel. At first many towns refused. Breck's council argued that its noon belonged to Breck, and some shopkeepers kept two clocks on the wall, one for local time and one for "railway time."

What finally settled the question was not an argument but a wire. In 1860, a telegraph line was strung beside the tracks, and each morning Ostrel sent a signal that let every station set its clock to the same second. Once railway time could be checked so easily, banks and post offices along the line adopted it, then schools, and finally the town councils themselves.

In 1874, Varland made a single national time official. By then the law only confirmed what daily life had already decided. The clock that people trusted most was no longer the sun over the square; it was the one that matched the train.$q$, 'draft')
