-- Passage rg10-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('60847744-bd46-53e5-8c50-5a712c327fd1', 'rg10-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Slow Start, Fast Finish$q$, $q$Many high school cross-country runners share a habit: they sprint the first few hundred meters of a race to grab a good position, then try to hold on. Coaches have long debated whether this strategy helps or hurts. A fictional study at the Marlow Sports Institute set out to answer the question with numbers rather than opinions.

The researchers, led by Dr. Keiko Brandt, asked forty runners from four schools to run the same five-kilometer course twice, one week apart. In one race, runners started at their normal fast pace. In the other, they were told to run the first kilometer about five percent slower than their goal pace and then speed up.

The results surprised several coaches. Thirty-one of the forty runners finished faster after the slow start, by an average of eighteen seconds. The gains came almost entirely in the final two kilometers, where the fast starters had slowed sharply.

Brandt's explanation connects the start of the race to its end. A hard early sprint makes the muscles burn energy faster than the body can resupply it, so waste products build up and the runner tires early. A slightly slower start keeps that buildup under control, which leaves more energy for the last stretch.

The study has limits. It included only forty runners, and all of them ran on a flat course in mild weather. Brandt suggests that coaches try the method in practice before using it in a race. Still, her findings give coaches a reason to question a habit that many runners had simply accepted.$q$, 'draft')
