-- Passage rg10-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('95f8d987-0aa8-5210-8647-ad01bca73e0d', 'rg10-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Harvesting Winter$q$, $q$Before electric refrigerators, the fictional lakeside town of Wexmoor earned much of its living from cold. Each January, when the ice on Lake Tamsin grew thick enough to hold a team of horses, workers cut it into large blocks and stored them for summer.

The process followed a careful order. First, crews scraped the snow off the ice so it would freeze deeper and clearer. Next, they marked a grid on the surface and cut along the lines with long saws. The floating blocks were then guided through a channel of open water to the shore, where a ramp lifted them into tall wooden icehouses.

Storing the ice was as important as cutting it. Workers packed each layer of blocks in thick sawdust, which trapped cold air and kept warm air out. Packed this way, a full icehouse could last until the next winter, losing only about a third of its ice to melting.

By the 1880s, Wexmoor ice was shipped by rail to cities two hundred kilometers away, where it cooled food in shops and homes. The trade depended on one condition, though: a cold winter. In 1890, a mild season left the lake ice too thin to cut. The cities, short of ice, turned to a new kind of machine that made ice from water using steam power.

Those machines never went away. Even when cold winters returned, city buyers chose factory ice, which arrived on schedule and did not depend on the weather. Wexmoor's icehouses closed one by one, and today the lake that once cooled a region is best known for its winter skating races.$q$, 'draft')
