-- Passage rg10-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('072a4e35-2450-5d97-981c-dda4c7f03dc7', 'rg10-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How a Mountain Range Divides the Rain$q$, $q$Travelers who cross the fictional Serran Range notice a sudden change. On the western slopes, thick forests of fir and cedar drip with moisture. Only sixty kilometers east, on the other side of the peaks, the land is dry grassland and scrub. The two sides share the same mountains and the same latitude, yet one receives five times as much rain as the other.

The explanation begins over the ocean to the west. Winds there pick up moisture as water evaporates from the sea surface, and they carry this damp air steadily eastward toward the coast.

When the air reaches the Serran Range, it has nowhere to go but up. As it rises, it expands and cools, and cool air cannot hold as much water vapor as warm air. The extra moisture condenses into clouds, and the clouds release it as rain and snow on the western slopes. By the time the air crosses the highest ridges, it has lost most of its water.

On the eastern side, the air sinks back down. Sinking air is squeezed and warms as it descends, so instead of forming clouds, it soaks up what little moisture remains in the land below. Scientists call the dry area this process creates a rain shadow.

The pattern shapes the lives of the people on each side. Western towns such as Lindqvist manage heavy runoff and run sawmills supplied by the forests. Eastern ranches around Dorrego depend on wells and on the snowmelt that flows down from the peaks each spring, which means the dry side still relies on the same storms that soaked the wet side months before.$q$, 'draft')
