-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('731f37bb-eb89-5cb4-98c8-2dc7b0f84d38', 'rg10-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Living Signs on the Bark$q$, $q$Look closely at an old stone wall or the bark of a tree, and you may notice crusty patches of gray, green, or orange. These are lichens, and although they look like a single plant, each one is actually a partnership between two very different organisms: a fungus and a tiny alga. The fungus provides shelter and holds onto moisture, while the alga uses sunlight to make food that both partners share.

In some ways, lichens are remarkably resilient. They survive on bare rock in freezing mountains and in deserts that see rain only a few times a year. When conditions are harsh, a lichen can dry out completely, then recover within minutes once water returns.

In another way, however, lichens are fragile. Because they have no roots, they absorb water and nutrients directly from the air and rain. Whatever is floating in the air goes straight into their tissues, so they are highly susceptible to pollution. A level of smoke or exhaust that a tree could tolerate may kill the lichens growing on its trunk.

That weakness makes lichens useful to scientists. Researchers treat them as indicators of air quality, living signs that reveal how clean or dirty the air in a place has been. A city park whose trees are covered in leafy, branching lichens probably has fairly clean air. Conversely, a park where only a few flat, crusty kinds survive likely has a pollution problem.

Students can join this work with little equipment. Some schools ask classes to survey the lichens on nearby trees each spring, recording which kinds appear and how much bark they cover. Over several years, these modest surveys, which need only a notebook and a hand lens, can show whether a neighborhood's air is improving or getting worse.$q$, 'draft')
