-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1d9751b5-86a6-595d-9244-70f23204d3ab', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Pacing the Long Row$q$, $q$Every spring, the rowing coaches at Delmont Ridge High School noticed the same pattern at their two-kilometer races. Their rowers would burst off the starting line, lead for the first few hundred meters and then fade badly in the final stretch. Coach Ingrid Halvorsen suspected the problem was pacing rather than fitness.

To test her idea, she used the rowing machines in the team's training room, which display each rower's speed and power on a screen. For three weeks, half the team trained with a target pace shown on the display and were told to hold it evenly from start to finish. The other half trained as usual, rowing as hard as they felt they could. At the end of the three weeks, every rower completed a timed two-kilometer test on the machines.

The even-pace group finished, on average, eleven seconds faster than they had before the experiment. The other group improved by only three seconds. Halvorsen also reviewed the power readings and saw that the even-pace rowers produced less power in the first minute than the other group, but they kept much more of their power in the last minute.

Several rowers said the even pace felt too slow at the start, and two admitted they ignored the screen during the first test. Halvorsen did not count those two in her results. This season, she plans to have the whole team practice even pacing on the water, where wind and current make a steady speed harder to judge than on a machine.$q$, 'draft')
