-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3f359569-85c4-592a-a1f7-a3538eb0a627', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving the Faded Banner$q$, $q$For nearly a century, a silk banner hung in the entrance hall of the Marrowfield Guild of Weavers. Painted with the guild's symbol, a golden shuttle crossed by two threads, it greeted every visitor. By 2018, however, the gold had faded to a dull tan, and the silk had split in dozens of places.

The guild hired textile conservator Lucía Abernethy-Okafor to examine it. She began by studying the banner under a microscope and found that the silk fibers had become brittle. The damage was worst on the side that had faced a tall window, while a strip near the bottom edge, which had been hidden behind a wooden rail, kept nearly its original color. Abernethy-Okafor explained that sunlight breaks down both silk and many dyes, and that the hidden strip showed what the whole banner once looked like.

Repainting the faded areas was never considered. Conservators generally try to preserve what remains rather than replace it, so that future researchers can study the original materials. Instead, Abernethy-Okafor sewed the split silk onto a backing of fine, dyed netting using stitches thinner than a human hair. The work took eleven months.

The banner now hangs in a room without windows, under lights that switch on only when a visitor enters. A printed reproduction, made from photographs of the hidden strip, hangs in the old entrance hall so that guests can see the bright gold the original once showed. Some members admit that they miss seeing the real banner each day, but most agree that it would not survive another century by the window.$q$, 'draft')
