-- Passage rg10-evidence-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ce57dd0a-cfa3-5c5e-aba6-67dac5acbde4', 'rg10-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Keep the Old Depot Open to the Public$q$, $q$The brick freight depot on Wexley Street has stood empty since the last cargo train left in 1994. Next month, the town council will decide whether to sell it to a developer who plans to tear it down for a parking garage. I believe the council should instead turn the depot into a public hall, and the evidence supports that choice.

First, Wexley lacks a large indoor space for community events. Last winter, the youth orchestra had to cancel two concerts because the school auditorium was booked for testing. The farmers market closes from November to April because no building can hold its forty stalls. The depot's main room is larger than the auditorium and has high windows that let in daylight.

Second, the building is sturdier than many people assume. In 2023, an engineering firm hired by the town found that the walls and roof beams were sound and that most repairs would involve the windows, wiring and heating. The firm estimated those repairs at about half the price the developer has offered for the land.

Supporters of the garage argue that downtown needs more parking. That concern is real on Saturdays, but a town count last spring found that the existing lots were less than sixty percent full on weekdays. Building an entire garage for a few busy hours a week makes little sense when a shuttle from the half-empty lot near the library could solve the problem.

Once the depot is gone, it cannot be brought back. The council should choose the option that serves residents every season of the year.$q$, 'draft')
